#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716758 :question/problem "![Circle centered at O(0,0), passing through the top point A(0,2), with an arrow showing clockwise motion.](images/5f/5fec78eab6df87be10bae2cea167929b6bb0194a00b8db6083b8ca44d996111e.png)\n\nThe circle shown is centered at $O(0,0)$ and passes through $A(0,2)$. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse this circle once clockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The distance from $O(0,0)$ to $A(0,2)$ is $2$, so the radius is $2$.\n\nTo start at $(0,2)$ when $\\theta=0$, use sine for the $x$-coordinate and cosine for the $y$-coordinate:\n\n$$\nx=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFor a small positive $\\theta$, $x$ becomes positive and $y$ decreases from $2$, so the point moves right from the top, in the clockwise direction. The quarter-turn positions are\n\n$$\n(0,2)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (2,0)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (0,-2)\\ \\longrightarrow\\ (-2,0).\n$$\n\nAlso, $x^2+y^2=4(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta)=4$, so the point stays on the required circle. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full turn without repeating the starting point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716763 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, with radius 2, starting at A(2,0) and directed counterclockwise.](images/15/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $A(2,0)$ when $\\theta=0$. Use one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The standard parametrization of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin is\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIt starts at $(r,0)$ and moves counterclockwise. With $r=2$, the required equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, the point is $(2,0)$. At $\\theta=\\pi/2$, it is $(0,2)$, confirming counterclockwise motion. As $\\theta$ increases through one full period, the circle is traversed once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716773 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, generate a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $r=2$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nBoth coordinates have amplitude $2$, so the radius is $2$. Unequal amplitudes would produce an ellipse, and equal amplitudes of a different size would give the wrong radius."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716779 :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at $(-3,4)$?" :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $r$ centered at $(a,b)$ has the parametrization\n\n$$\nx=a+r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b+r\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $r=2$, $a=-3$, and $b=4$, so\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta-3,\\qquad y=2\\sin\\theta+4.\n$$\n\nBoth trigonometric coefficients must have magnitude $2$; the constant terms give the center $(-3,4)$. Reversing the signs of those constants changes the center, interchanging them swaps its coordinates, and using $4$ as both coefficients gives radius $4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716781 :question/problem "![Circle centered at C(-2,1), with P(1,5) on its circumference and A at its rightmost point.](images/41/41afde8ef1206982455ac09b33650bd0a842d2ac50b1001bab590470404c402e.png)\n\nA circle has center $C(-2,1)$ and passes through $P(1,5)$. The point $A$ is its rightmost point. Construct parametric equations for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ that traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\cos\\theta$ in the $x$-equation and $\\sin\\theta$ in the $y$-equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius is the distance from the center $C$ to the point $P$:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{(1-(-2))^2+(5-1)^2}=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5.\n$$\n\nA circle of radius $r$ centered at $(a,b)$ has the parametrization\n\n$$\nx=a+r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b+r\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWith $r=5$ and $(a,b)=(-2,1)$, the required equations are\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta-2,\\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta+1.\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, the point is $(3,1)$, the rightmost point $A$. At $\\theta=\\pi/2$, it is $(-2,6)$, above the center; thus the motion is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ gives exactly one traversal. Using $25$ in place of $5$ would confuse the squared radius with the radius."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716790 :question/problem "![Circle centered at C(-2,3) with radius 4 and rightmost point A(2,3).](images/78/78429ab38480bd45ed608be7977f3102cfad84b9a190a61322ab25235ad2d7a0.png)\n\nThe circle shown has radius $4$ and center $C(-2,3)$. Construct parametric equations for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ that traverse it once counterclockwise, starting at $A(2,3)$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\cos\\theta$ in the $x$-equation and $\\sin\\theta$ in the $y$-equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $r$ centered at $(a,b)$ has the parametrization\n\n$$\nx=a+r\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b+r\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSubstitute $r=4$ and $(a,b)=(-2,3)$:\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta-2,\\qquad y=4\\sin\\theta+3.\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, this gives $(x,y)=(2,3)=A$. As $\\theta$ increases toward $\\pi/2$, the point moves upward to $(-2,7)$, so the traversal is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716796 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=7\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side." :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=7\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{7\\sqrt{3}}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{7\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{147}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=3\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{2}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{y^2}{18}&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{147}+\\frac{y^2}{18}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{147}+\\frac{y^2}{18}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716802 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{5}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{4}{3}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{3}{5}\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{5}{3}x&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{5}{3}x\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{25}{9}x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{4}{3}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{\\frac{4}{3}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{\\frac{4}{3}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{9}{16}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716804 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{6}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{36}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\sin\\theta\\\\y&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(y\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{36}+y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{36}+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716812 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{7}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{1}{5}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{1}{7}\\cos\\theta\\\\7x&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(7x\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\49x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{5}\\sin\\theta\\\\5y&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(5y\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\25y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n49x^2+25y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n49x^2+25y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716821 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sqrt{5}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side." :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sqrt{5}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{5}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{y^2}{80}&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{80}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{80}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716918 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\theta-2,\\quad y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sin\\theta+1,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x+$ {{field-1}} $)^2+(y-$ {{field-2}} $)^2=$ {{field-3}}.\n\nFill in the three missing values." :question/worked-solution "First undo the translations, then isolate the trigonometric functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+2&=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\theta &\\quad \\frac{2}{3}(x+2)&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny-1&=\\frac{3}{2}\\sin\\theta &\\quad \\frac{2}{3}(y-1)&=\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare each equation and add. Using the Pythagorean identity gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{9}(x+2)^2+\\frac{4}{9}(y-1)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{4}{9}\\bigl((x+2)^2+(y-1)^2\\bigr)&=1\\\\\n(x+2)^2+(y-1)^2&=\\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe three missing values are $2$, $1$, and $\\frac{9}{4}$, in that order. The center is $(-2,1)$ and the radius is $\\frac{3}{2}$. The signs already printed in the answer agree with subtracting the center coordinates. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716924 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nDividing each equation by $2$ and squaring gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}=\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad \\frac{y^2}{4}=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nUse $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$ to eliminate $\\theta$. Choose the Cartesian equation." :question/worked-solution "Add the two supplied equations and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{4}&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\n\\frac{x^2+y^2}{4}&=1\\\\\nx^2+y^2&=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2=4$. The radius is $2$, so its square is $4$, not $2$ or $16$. The squared terms are added, not subtracted. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716934 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Eliminate $\\theta$ using $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nSquaring the $x$-equation and the $y$-equation gives\n\n$$\nx^2=\\frac{9}{4}\\cos^2\\theta,\\qquad y^2=\\frac{9}{4}\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nAdd the equations and factor out the common coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{9}{4}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot1\\\\\n&=\\frac{9}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{4}$. Both numerator and denominator of the radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ are squared. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716935 :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(C)$, given that\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$\\det(C)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, multiply the main diagonal entries and subtract the product of the off-diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nEach diagonal contains two negative entries, so both products are positive. Keep the order: main-diagonal product minus off-diagonal product.\n\nThe determinant can be negative; determinant bars do not mean absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(C) &=(-2)(-5)-(-3)(-4) \\\\ &=10-12 \\\\ &=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716938 :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A)$, given that\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, multiply the main diagonal entries and subtract the product of the off-diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\n![The blue main diagonal gives ad; subtract bc from the red off-diagonal. ](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(A) &=(2)(4)-(1)(3) \\\\ &=8-3 \\\\ &=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716945 :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(B)$, given that\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, multiply the main diagonal entries and subtract the product of the off-diagonal entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nThe off-diagonal product is negative: $(3)(-4)=-12$. Subtracting this negative product adds $12$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\det(B) &=(2)(1)-(3)(-4) \\\\ &=2-(-12) \\\\ &=2+12 \\\\ &=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716965 :question/problem "![](images/50/503814b9764dae553ac77b9db5fd393ea93cf02a86989afc59551b1f83ee32a5.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (3,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-3,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (3,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the lower-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,-6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(3,0)$ when $\\theta=2\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/4e/4e165eb4bd24aa6656234b1f4180e37ae090e929d1d56906d57e5735ab09c73e.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716970 :question/problem "![](images/25/25239827db5874fa78a62a843d8775a2f619d9e73112bc28f9d5c9d3cda0792c.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $1$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (2,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,1) \\\\ \\pi & (-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-1) \\\\ 2\\pi & (2,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(2,0)$ when $\\theta=0$ and ends at $(0,1)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/9c/9cc6d14cd425659b343c2e97ff9142f2a88e89862c494e9031bb2dbced4d6f25.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716972 :question/problem "![](images/70/70623a34ef6d5bbddee50ea91c0d640d83e08b8f1396222cd161987379498fd1.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=8\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $8$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (6,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,8) \\\\ \\pi & (-6,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-8) \\\\ 2\\pi & (6,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the lower part relative to the center. It starts at $(-6,0)$ when $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $(6,0)$ when $\\theta=2\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/3c/3c20a9561220717844184c0e5570e7838d6d237ae3ac9415d41a3a76161f65f6.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=8\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\pi,2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716974 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcaeffe2f84a5dfd04c64c9947b1240b0275241c5ab5c3276aa778a830ecba54.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $7$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (4,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,7) \\\\ \\pi & (-4,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-7) \\\\ 2\\pi & (4,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(0,-7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/a7/a7543597e30e12a24ed370c2cafeae050ad74706b5718c048ec43463521f46aa.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716977 :question/problem "![](images/97/9711e8b2a21b2888a077a0c12dde13de2719ce314c1379b1319e4363df529e16.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (5,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-5,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (5,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(-5,0)$ when $\\theta=\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/33/33bf8513f5b535844e3841f1f1c01a6ffd3718dda7cf0f609734d883306623e1.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716984 :question/problem "The nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ start at $O,$ with $|\\mathbf{a}|=8$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=6\\sqrt{2}.$ The diagram shows their counterclockwise angles from the same reference ray $r.$\n\n![Vectors a and b at counterclockwise angles of 30 degrees and 150 degrees from ray r, respectively.](images/70/7039bc30f707124fe8fa0a4167a0ef1979acaae13e0d39c2769c298f6af6bd8c.png)\n\nFind the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both directions are measured counterclockwise from the same ray, so the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta=150^\\circ-30^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThe vector being projected is $\\mathbf{b},$ whose magnitude is $6\\sqrt{2}.$ The direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ determines the projection axis, but its magnitude $8$ does not enter the formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n&=|\\mathbf{b}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\sqrt{2}\\cos120^\\circ \\\\\n&=6\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe obtuse angle gives a negative scalar projection. Reversing which vector is projected generally changes the answer: using $8$ here would instead calculate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716987 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{a}|=6$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta=60^\\circ.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=|\\mathbf{a}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\cos60^\\circ \\\\\n&=6\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe projection is positive because the angle between the vectors is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716989 :question/problem "The vectors below have magnitudes $|\\mathbf{a}|=8$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=5.$ The dashed ray points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b};$ the marked $30^\\circ$ angle is between that ray and $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n![Vectors a and b with a 30-degree angle between a and the ray opposite b.](images/03/0378d71954ac8d6821e8db82e47a1ac90cebb9cbf4589b1edd1df26ba1bade2c.png)\n\nFind the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The marked angle is not the angle between the two vectors. Since the dashed ray and $\\mathbf{b}$ point in opposite directions, the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta=180^\\circ-30^\\circ=150^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe are projecting $\\mathbf{a},$ so we use $|\\mathbf{a}|=8.$ The magnitude of $\\mathbf{b}$ is not needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=|\\mathbf{a}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=8\\cos150^\\circ \\\\\n&=8\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative sign indicates that the component of $\\mathbf{a}$ along the line of $\\mathbf{b}$ points opposite to $\\mathbf{b}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717026 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-3,6\\rangle$. Find $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}-\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both projections use the same dot product, but their denominators differ:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}&=1(2)+2(-3)+2(6)=8,\\\\\n\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}=3,\\\\\n\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-3)^2+6^2}=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}&=\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert}=\\frac{8}{7},\\\\\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}&=\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract in the requested order:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}-\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n=\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{8}{3}\n=\\frac{24-56}{21}\n=-\\frac{32}{21}.\n$$\n\nThe dot product is symmetric; the scalar projection generally is not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717027 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle$. Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "The vector we project onto is $\\mathbf{b}$, so its magnitude is the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2(1)+(-1)(2)+1(2)}{\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2-2+2}{\\sqrt{9}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717031 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle t,2,-1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-1,2\\rangle$, where $t$ is real. Given that $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}=-2$, find the exact value of $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First use the given projection to determine the missing component. Since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2+2^2}=3,\\\\\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}&=2t+2(-1)+(-1)(2)=2t-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2t-4}{3}&=-2,\\\\\n2t-4&=-6,\\\\\nt&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle-1,2,-1\\rangle$. The reverse projection uses the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{6},\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}&=2(-1)+(-1)(2)+2(-1)=-6,\\\\\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n&=\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert}\n=\\frac{-6}{\\sqrt{6}}\n=-\\sqrt{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing the projection keeps the dot product but changes the denominator, so the answer need not equal the given $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717046 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(0, 11), moving counterclockwise.](images/9f/9f3f09db56a7b67f79116f831677f9b1dc749a74d0631ca80dbd67d4b96d16a2.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $11$ centered at the origin, shown above. Which set of parametric equations will traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A(0,11)$ when $\\theta=0$? Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation." :question/worked-solution "The supplied radius is $11$, so each coordinate has amplitude $11$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(0,11)$, use sine in the $x$-coordinate and cosine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(-11,0)$, moving counterclockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=-11\\sin\\theta\\\\\ny&=11\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(0,11)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(-11,0)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $11$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717054 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(20, 0), moving clockwise.](images/30/300f62a9c5ffd6701e0ee76cb1a690ec27fe310ead64ef0c1907f8cd3d1c32d3.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $20$ centered at the origin, shown above. Which set of parametric equations will traverse the circle once clockwise, starting at $A(20,0)$ when $\\theta=0$? Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation." :question/worked-solution "The supplied radius is $20$, so each coordinate has amplitude $20$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(20,0)$, use cosine in the $x$-coordinate and sine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(0,-20)$, moving clockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=20\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny&=-20\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(20,0)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(0,-20)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $20$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717058 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(0, 17), moving counterclockwise.](images/24/2437687777b9e8430004201f2f8deda99facd46a839543192e592b095a00a9af.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $17$ centered at the origin, shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A(0,17)$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The supplied radius is $17$, so each coordinate has amplitude $17$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(0,17)$, use sine in the $x$-coordinate and cosine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(-17,0)$, moving counterclockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=-17\\sin\\theta\\\\\ny&=17\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(0,17)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(-17,0)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $17$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717065 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(5, 0), moving clockwise.](images/d2/d2a030f351af3eb4d40eed5b0efffd8957b8edb3b67a2fb5de3c99d23da4eb2f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin, shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once clockwise, starting at $A(5,0)$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The supplied radius is $5$, so each coordinate has amplitude $5$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(5,0)$, use cosine in the $x$-coordinate and sine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(0,-5)$, moving clockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=5\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny&=-5\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(5,0)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(0,-5)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $5$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717067 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(0, 20), moving clockwise.](images/d6/d6f4d2dd7871e81760eea8cce41badd5e0cc1175708dcb5312920c721f71300d.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $20$ centered at the origin, shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once clockwise, starting at $A(0,20)$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The supplied radius is $20$, so each coordinate has amplitude $20$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(0,20)$, use sine in the $x$-coordinate and cosine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(20,0)$, moving clockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=20\\sin\\theta\\\\\ny&=20\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(0,20)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(20,0)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $20$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717090 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(-2, 0), moving counterclockwise.](images/b9/b909c3c1cbd9961d3c0d5c38fec91131d063c52b0674eea4a952911b59a97617.png)\n\nThe circle shown is centered at $O(0,0)$ and passes through the labeled point $A$. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The axis point is $A(-2,0)$. Its distance from $O(0,0)$ is $2$, so the radius is $2$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(-2,0)$, use cosine in the $x$-coordinate and sine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(0,-2)$, moving counterclockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=-2\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny&=-2\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(-2,0)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(0,-2)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $2$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717092 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(0, -20), moving counterclockwise.](images/7e/7e8459d554d63a52cdf1e506727d1d31ee8920454bdc32a9b5a79140a967c35c.png)\n\nThe circle shown is centered at $O(0,0)$ and passes through the labeled point $A$. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The axis point is $A(0,-20)$. Its distance from $O(0,0)$ is $20$, so the radius is $20$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(0,-20)$, use sine in the $x$-coordinate and cosine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(20,0)$, moving counterclockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=20\\sin\\theta\\\\\ny&=-20\\cos\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(0,-20)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(20,0)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $20$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717094 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(15, 0), moving clockwise.](images/fb/fbd5852fb6bf6a13f9f113442ec28347ad97d0924a4671e42ed6eef09b93aa14.png)\n\nThe circle shown is centered at $O(0,0)$ and passes through the labeled point $A$. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once clockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The axis point is $A(15,0)$. Its distance from $O(0,0)$ is $15$, so the radius is $15$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(15,0)$, use cosine in the $x$-coordinate and sine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(0,-15)$, moving clockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=15\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny&=-15\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(15,0)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(0,-15)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $15$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717096 :question/problem "![Circle centered at the origin, starting at A(20, 0), moving counterclockwise.](images/80/80c65d1f12cb7b212604b54bf17d5c007e702756e4cea6d43bab4a5f7ddb383d.png)\n\nThe circle shown is centered at $O(0,0)$ and passes through the labeled point $A$. Construct a set of parametric equations that traverse the circle once counterclockwise, starting at $A$ when $\\theta=0$. Use $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ and one sine or cosine term with argument $\\theta$ in each equation.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}, $y=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The axis point is $A(20,0)$. Its distance from $O(0,0)$ is $20$, so the radius is $20$.\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$. To start at $A(20,0)$, use cosine in the $x$-coordinate and sine in the $y$-coordinate. Choose the signs so that the next quarter-turn reaches $(0,20)$, moving counterclockwise.\n\nThus the equations are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=20\\cos\\theta\\\\\ny&=20\\sin\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$ the point is $(20,0)$; at $\\theta=\\pi/2$ it is $(0,20)$, confirming the start and direction. Both coordinate amplitudes are $20$, so the point remains on the required circle. As $\\theta$ runs through $[0,2\\pi)$, one full period traces the circle exactly once."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717104 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=6\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{6}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=6\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{6}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{36}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{36}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $36$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{36}x^2+\\frac{1}{36}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{1}{36}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=36$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $6$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717105 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{9}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{9}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{9}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{9}x&=\\sin\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{9}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{9}y&=\\cos\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{81}x^2&=\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{81}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{81}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{81}x^2+\\frac{4}{81}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{81}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{81}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{81}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{9}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717106 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Undo any translations and divide by the common radius to isolate the trigonometric terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{11}{2}\\cos\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}x&=\\cos\\theta \\\\ \ny&=\\frac{11}{2}\\sin\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{2}{11}y&=\\sin\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquare both normalized equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$, then multiply by $\\frac{121}{4}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4}{121}x^2+\\frac{4}{121}y^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta \\\\ \n\\frac{4}{121}\\bigl(x^2+y^2\\bigr)&=1 \\\\ \nx^2+y^2&=\\frac{121}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $x^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{121}{4}$. The center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $\\frac{11}{2}$. The full parameter interval traces the entire circle."}]}
